e>FUJIMURA KOJI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>KUROKI TERUHISA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>OHASHI KOICHI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>TAKASHIMA TSUTOMU</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>TOKUMOTO YUICHI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>FUJIMURA, KOJI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KUROKI, TERUHISA</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>OHASHI, KOICHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TAKASHIMA, TSUTOMU</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TOKUMOTO, YUICHI</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>An efficient dehalogenation method which comprises treating an organic compound containing a compound of a halogen, such as fluorine or chlorine, as an impurity and having unconjugated carbon-carbon double bonds, for example, a butene polymer produced with a boron trifluoride catalyst, with an inorganic solid treating agent containing one or more aluminum atoms. When the dehalogenation is conducted in the presence of a basic susbtance such as ammonia or an amine, it can be continued over long while inhibiting the isomerization of the unconjugated carbon-carbon double bonds.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un procédé de déshalogénation efficace, ce procédé consistant notamment à traiter, au moyen d'un agent de traitement solide inorganique contenant un ou plusieurs atomes d'aluminium, un composé organique renfermant une impureté comme un composé d'un halogène, par exemple du fluor ou du chlore, et présentant des doubles liaisons carbone-carbone non conjuguées, par exemple un polymère buténique produit à l'aide d'un catalyseur de trifluorure de bore. Une fois cette déshalogénation effectuée en présence d'une substance basique comme l'ammoniac ou une amine, elle peut se prolonger de manière à inhiber l'isomérisation des doubles liaisons carbone-carbone non conjuguées.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>